    <section class="empire-glance" id="at-a-glance" aria-labelledby="glance-title">
      <div class="empire-glance-main"><h2 class="empire-glance-title" id="glance-title">At a glance</h2><ul><li>Born in Caprese in 1475 and raised in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/florence">Florence</a>, Michelangelo was apprenticed to Domenico Ghirlandaio at 13 and joined the household of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lorenzo-de-medici">Lorenzo de' Medici</a>, and he carved the Pietà and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/david">David</a> before the age of 30.</li><li>For popes Julius II, Clement VII, Paul III and others he painted the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sistine-chapel-ceiling">Sistine Chapel ceiling</a> and The Last Judgment and designed the Medici Chapel, the Laurentian Library and the plan of St Peter's, while the tomb of Julius II occupied him for forty years and was never finished to his satisfaction.</li><li>He was the first Western artist whose biography was published during his lifetime, and he also wrote more than three hundred poems, whose love for men later generations edited, allegorised or accepted at face value.</li></ul></div>
      <aside class="empire-dyk"><p class="empire-dyk-title">Did you know?</p><p>The Pietà is the only work known to have been signed by Michelangelo: his name is carved on Mary's sash.</p></aside>
    </section>

