    <section class="rise-fall" id="timeline" aria-labelledby="timeline-title">
      <h2 class="profile-section-title" id="timeline-title">Timeline</h2>
      <ol class="rf-events"><li><span class="rf-year">1475</span><span class="rf-text">Born on 6 March in Caprese, near <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/arezzo">Arezzo</a></span></li><li><span class="rf-year">1488</span><span class="rf-text">Apprenticed at 13 to Domenico Ghirlandaio in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/florence">Florence</a></span></li><li><span class="rf-year">1499</span><span class="rf-text">Completes the Pietà in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a>, at 24, and returns to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/florence">Florence</a></span></li><li><span class="rf-year">1504</span><span class="rf-text">Completes the statue of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/david">David</a>, begun 40 years earlier by Agostino di Duccio</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">1512</span><span class="rf-text">Finishes the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sistine-chapel-ceiling">Sistine Chapel ceiling</a>, painted over about four years from 1508</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">1541</span><span class="rf-text">Completes The Last Judgment on the altar wall of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sistine-chapel">Sistine Chapel</a>, begun in 1534</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">1546</span><span class="rf-text">Appointed architect of St Peter's Basilica at the age of 71</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">1564</span><span class="rf-text">Dies in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a> on 18 February, aged 88; he is buried in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/florence">Florence</a></span></li></ol>
    </section>


    <div class="profile-section-block" id="early">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Early Life</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/giotto"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/giotto.jpg" alt="Giotto" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/giotto">Giotto</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Giotto,_Santa_Maria_Novella.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Sailko, CC BY 3.0</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>Michelangelo di Lodovico Buonarroti Simoni was born on 6 March 1475 in Caprese, a small town in Valtiberina near <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/arezzo">Arezzo</a>. His family had for several generations been small-scale bankers in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/florence">Florence</a>, but the bank failed, and his father Ludovico briefly took a government post in Caprese, where he was the town's judicial administrator. His mother was Francesca di Neri del Miniato di Siena. The family claimed descent from the Countess Matilde di Canossa, a claim that remains unproven but which Michelangelo believed. The family returned to Florence a few months after his birth. After his mother's long illness and her death in 1481, when he was six, he lived with a nanny and her husband, a stonecutter, in Settignano, where his father owned a marble quarry and a small farm. There, according to his own words as Vasari quotes them, he received with his nurse's milk the knack of handling chisel and hammer.</p><p>He was sent to Florence to study grammar under the Humanist Francesco da Urbino but showed no interest, preferring to copy paintings from churches and to keep company with other painters. Florence was then <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italy">Italy</a>'s greatest centre of the arts and learning, its art sponsored by the Signoria, the merchant <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/guild">guilds</a> and patrons such as the Medici. He studied and copied in drawings the frescoes of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/giotto">Giotto</a> and of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/masaccio">Masaccio</a> in the Brancacci Chapel.</p><p>In 1488, at 13, he was apprenticed to Domenico Ghirlandaio, who had the largest workshop in Florence. The next year his father persuaded Ghirlandaio to pay him as an artist, which was rare for someone so young.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="rise">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Rise</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/lorenzo-de-medici"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/lorenzo-de-medici.jpg" alt="Lorenzo de' Medici" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/lorenzo-de-medici">Lorenzo de' Medici</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Lorenzo_de_Medici.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Workshop of Bronzino, Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>In 1489 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lorenzo-de-medici">Lorenzo de' Medici</a>, the de facto ruler of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/florence">Florence</a>, asked Ghirlandaio for his two best pupils, and Ghirlandaio sent Michelangelo and Francesco Granacci. From 1490 to 1492 Michelangelo attended the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/platonic-academy">Platonic Academy</a> founded by the Medici, where <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/marsilio-ficino">Marsilio Ficino</a>, Pico della <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mirandola">Mirandola</a> and Poliziano were among those who influenced his work and outlook. He carved the reliefs Madonna of the Stairs and Battle of the Centaurs, the latter on a theme suggested by Poliziano and commissioned by Lorenzo. At 17 he was struck on the nose by a fellow pupil, Pietro Torrigiano, which caused the disfigurement seen in his portraits.</p><p>Lorenzo's death on 8 April 1492 ended this security. Michelangelo went back to his father's house and carved a wooden Crucifix for the prior of Santo Spirito, whose hospital let him study anatomy on corpses, the first of several occasions in his career when he dissected. In 1494 the Medici were expelled from Florence with the rise of Savonarola, and he went to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/venice">Venice</a> and then <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bologna">Bologna</a>, where he carved small figures for the Shrine of St Dominic. Back in Florence he carved a child St <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/john-the-baptist">John the Baptist</a> and a sleeping Cupid. According to Condivi, a Medici patron had the Cupid made to look buried so it could be passed off as ancient; the buyer, Cardinal Raffaele Riario, found out but was so impressed that he invited the artist to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a>. Michelangelo arrived there on 25 June 1496, aged 21.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="deeds">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">What They Did</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/sistine-chapel-ceiling"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/sistine-chapel-ceiling.jpg" alt="Sistine Chapel ceiling" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/sistine-chapel-ceiling">Sistine Chapel ceiling</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:CAPPELLA_SISTINA_Ceiling.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Michelangelo, CC BY-SA 3.0</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>In <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a> he carved a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dionysus">Bacchus</a> for Riario, which the cardinal rejected, and in 1497–98 was commissioned by the French ambassador, Cardinal Jean de Bilhères-Lagraulas, to carve a Pietà. He finished it at 24, and Vasari wrote that it was a miracle that a formless block of stone could be reduced to a perfection that nature can scarcely create in the flesh. Back in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/florence">Florence</a> in 1499, he completed the statue of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/david">David</a> in 1504, a colossal marble figure begun 40 years before by Agostino di Duccio and intended as a symbol of Florentine freedom; a committee including Botticelli and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/leonardo-da-vinci">Leonardo da Vinci</a> placed it in the Piazza della Signoria.</p><p>In 1505 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pope-julius-ii">Pope Julius II</a> commissioned a tomb to hold forty statues, to be finished in five years. Michelangelo kept being interrupted by other tasks, and worked on it for 40 years; its best-known figure is <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/moses">Moses</a>, completed in 1516. In 1508 he began the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sistine-chapel-ceiling">Sistine Chapel ceiling</a>. He had been asked to paint the twelve apostles; he persuaded the pope to give him a free hand and painted nine episodes from Genesis, with prophets and Sibyls on the pendentives. It covers more than 500 square metres and contains over 300 figures. It took about four years, to 1512.</p><p>From 1534 to October 1541 he painted The Last Judgment on the altar wall, for Clement VII and then Paul III. In 1546 he became architect of St Peter's and returned to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/donato-bramante">Bramante</a>'s idea of a centrally planned church.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="opponents">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Opponents &amp; Critics</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/donato-bramante"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/donato-bramante.jpg" alt="Donato Bramante" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/donato-bramante">Donato Bramante</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Donato_Bramante.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>Michelangelo had hot-tempered quarrels with patrons. On 17 April 1506 he left <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a> in secret after arguing with Julius II, and stayed in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/florence">Florence</a> until the Florentine government pressed him to go back. Condivi's account says that <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/donato-bramante">Bramante</a> resented the tomb commission and persuaded the pope to give Michelangelo the ceiling, in a medium he did not know, hoping he would fail. In a letter of late 1542, Michelangelo himself blamed the tension with Julius II on the envy of Bramante and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/raphael">Raphael</a>, saying of Raphael that all he had in art he got from him.</p><p>The Last Judgment drew objections. Showing Christ and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mary-mother-of-jesus">Virgin Mary</a> naked was thought sacrilegious by Cardinal Carafa and Monsignor Sernini, who campaigned for the fresco to be removed or censored; Paul III resisted. Shortly before Michelangelo's death, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/council-of-trent">Council of Trent</a> decided to cover the genitals, and Daniele da Volterra was commissioned to do so.</p><p>After the fall of the Florentine republic in 1530, where he had supported the republic and worked on its fortifications, Pope Clement, a Medici, sentenced Michelangelo to death; he was eventually pardoned. His biographer Paolo Giovio wrote that his nature was so rough and uncouth that his domestic habits were incredibly squalid and deprived posterity of pupils. His love poetry later troubled editors: his grandnephew published it in 1623 with the pronouns' gender changed, and the scholar Anthony Hughes argues that the poems to Tommaso dei Cavalieri cannot be taken as expressions of personal desire.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="last">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Last Years</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/palazzo-farnese"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/palazzo-farnese.jpg" alt="Palazzo Farnese" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/palazzo-farnese">Palazzo Farnese</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Palais_Farnese.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Myrabella, CC BY-SA 3.0</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>In 1527 the citizens, encouraged by the sack of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a>, threw out the Medici and restored the republic. A siege followed, and Michelangelo worked on the city's fortifications from 1528 to 1529. The city fell in 1530 and the Medici returned, with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alessandro-de-medici">Alessandro de' Medici</a> as first Duke of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/florence">Florence</a>. It is thought that Michelangelo hid for two months in a small chamber under the Medici chapels, drawing on the walls, in a room rediscovered in 1975. Pardoned, he left for Rome in 1534 and lived near Santa Maria di Loreto. There he met the poet Vittoria <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/colonna-family">Colonna</a>, who remained one of his closest friends until her death in 1547.</p><p>In his last decades he painted two frescoes in the Pauline Chapel, completed in 1550, and worked on architecture including the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/capitoline-hill">Campidoglio</a>, the upper floor of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/palazzo-farnese">Palazzo Farnese</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/porta-pia">Porta Pia</a>. He wrote that painting and sculpture could no longer calm his soul, which had turned to divine love. He made Pietàs in old age, and the last sculpture he worked on, six days before his death, was the Rondanini Pietà, which he carved away until too little stone remained to finish it.</p><p>He died in Rome on 18 February 1564, aged 88. His body was taken to Santa Croce in Florence, as he had asked. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cosimo-i-de-medici">Cosimo I de' Medici</a> organised a state funeral and supplied the marble for his tomb, designed by Vasari for his heir Lionardo and taking over 14 years to build.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="legacy">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Legacy</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/raphael"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/raphael.jpg" alt="Raphael" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/raphael">Raphael</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Raffaello_Sanzio.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Raphael, Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>Michelangelo was the first Western artist whose biography was published during his lifetime. Vasari's Lives of 1550 gave him as its only living subject, presented his work as the culmination of the revival of the arts and called him divino. Condivi's Life of 1553, prepared with Michelangelo's participation, corrected Vasari in places. Contemporaries admired his terribilità, the ability to inspire awe. Vasari wrote that the Sistine ceiling had been a beacon to painters, restoring light to a world long in darkness. Attempts by later artists to imitate the expressive physicality of his style contributed to the rise of Mannerism.</p><p>His works influenced artists for generations. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/raphael">Raphael</a> drew on his figures and prophets, Pontormo on the Last Judgment, and Rodin and Henry Moore on the unfinished Captives for Julius's tomb. The Laurentian Library vestibule was among the first buildings to use classical forms expressively; Nikolaus Pevsner saw in it Mannerism in its most sublime architectural form. The dome of St Peter's, completed after his death by Giacomo della Porta, influenced church building for centuries, including St Paul's Cathedral in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/london">London</a>.</p><p>He was a devout Catholic, and enrolled in the Secular Franciscan Order. He lived simply, saying that he had always lived like a poor man, though his net worth was about 50,000 gold ducats. Since John Addington Symonds translated his poems in 1893 with their original genders, more readers accept that they express a preference for young men.</p></div><details class="sec-src"><summary>Sources for this section</summary><p class="sec-src-n">Works that Wikipedia&#8217;s article &#8220;Michelangelo&#8221; cites where it covers Legacy:</p><ul><li>Cristina Acidini Luchinat, The Medici, Michelangelo, and the Art of Late Renaissance Florence (Yale University Press / Detroit Institute of Arts, 2002)</li></ul></details>
      </div>

    <p class="profile-summary" id="summary"><em>Michelangelo (1475–1564), born in Caprese and raised in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/florence">Florence</a>, carved the Pietà and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/david">David</a> before he was 30, painted the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sistine-chapel-ceiling">Sistine Chapel ceiling</a> and The Last Judgment, and became architect of St Peter's at 71. He also wrote over three hundred poems.</em></p>

